<display numerical data using dot plots, histograms & box plots>: Dot plots, histograms, and box plots are graphical representations used to display the distribution of numerical data, showing patterns such as central tendency, spread, and outliers.;
<describe the attributes of a data set>: Attributes of a data set refer to the individual characteristics, properties, or features that describe the data points, such as variables, columns, or fields in a dataset (IEEE Standard for Data Management, 2020).;
<give & describe quantitative measures of center & variability>: Quantitative measures of center and variability are statistical metrics used to summarize and describe the central tendency and spread of numerical data distributions.;
<relate measures of center & variablity to distribution of data>: Measures of center (e.g., mean, median) and variability (e.g., range, standard deviation) are statistical tools used to summarize and interpret the shape, spread, and central tendency of a data distribution.;
<apply properties of operations to create equivalent expressions>: The process of using mathematical properties—such as commutative, associative, distributive, identity, and inverse properties—to rewrite algebraic expressions in different but equivalent forms.;
<understand equivalent expressions in context>: Equivalent expressions are algebraic expressions that yield the same value for all values of the variables involved, and understanding them in context means recognizing their equivalence within a specific real-world or mathematical scenario.;
<solve applied problems using numerical and algebraic expressions and equations>: The process of formulating and solving mathematical problems using numerical values, algebraic expressions, and equations to model real-world situations and derive solutions.;
<solve 2-step equations>: Solving 2-step equations involves isolating the variable using two inverse operations (e.g., addition/subtraction followed by multiplication/division) to find the value of the unknown variable.;
<solve, graph & interpret 2-step inequalities>: Solving, graphing, and interpreting 2-step inequalities involves finding the range of values that satisfy a linear inequality requiring two operations (e.g., addition/subtraction and multiplication/division) to isolate the variable, then representing the solution on a number line and explaining its meaning in context.;
<solve scale drawing problems>: Solving scale drawing problems involves interpreting and applying scale factors to accurately represent real-world dimensions on a smaller or larger drawing, commonly used in architecture, engineering, and cartography.;
<draw geometric shapes & unique triangles>: Drawing geometric shapes involves representing figures like circles, triangles, rectangles, and polygons using precise lines, angles, and proportions; unique triangles refer to triangles that are determined by specific, non-redundant conditions (e.g., SSS, SAS, ASA) that yield only one possible triangle.;
<describe 2d cross sections of 3d figures>: A 2D cross section is the shape formed by the intersection of a plane with a 3D figure, revealing a two-dimensional slice of the original object.;
<solve problems involving area & circumference of circles>: The ability to calculate the area (A = πr²) and circumference (C = 2πr or C = πd) of circles using mathematical formulas, and apply these calculations to solve real-world and theoretical problems.;
<use facts about angles to write & solve equations>: The use of geometric properties of angles—such as supplementary, complementary, vertical, and adjacent angles—to formulate and solve algebraic equations that determine unknown angle measures.;
<find area, volume & surface area of 2d/3d objects>: Area, volume, and surface area are geometric measures used to quantify the size of two-dimensional and three-dimensional shapes, respectively. Area measures the extent of a 2D surface (e.g., square, circle), volume quantifies the space enclosed by a 3D object (e.g., cube, sphere), and surface area calculates the total area of the outer surfaces of a 3D object.;
<describe situations where opposite quantities combine to make 0>: Opposite quantities combine to make 0 when two values of equal magnitude but opposite signs are added, resulting in a sum of zero. This concept is foundational in understanding additive inverses in mathematics.;
<interpret sums of rational numbers>: Interpreting sums of rational numbers involves understanding and representing the addition of fractions, decimals, and integers (which are all rational numbers) in meaningful contexts, such as real-world problems or number line models.;
<understand properties of subtracting rational numbers>: Subtracting rational numbers involves finding the difference between two numbers expressible as fractions (a/b and c/d, where b, d ≠ 0), following arithmetic rules that preserve closure, commutativity, and associativity in specific contexts.;
<apply properties of operations to add/subtract rational numbers>: The application of properties of operations—such as commutative, associative, and distributive properties—to add and subtract rational numbers, which include fractions, decimals, and integers that can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.;
<understand applied context for multiplying rational numbers>: Multiplying rational numbers in applied contexts involves using the mathematical operation of multiplication on fractions or decimals (rational numbers) to solve real-world problems, such as scaling quantities, calculating proportions, or determining areas and rates.;
<understand applied context for dividing rational numbers>: Dividing rational numbers involves determining how many times one fraction or decimal (expressed as a ratio of two integers) fits into another, with applications in real-world scenarios requiring proportional reasoning.;
<apply properties of operations to multiply/divide rational numbers>: The application of fundamental properties of operations—such as commutative, associative, distributive, identity, and inverse properties—to multiply and divide rational numbers (fractions, decimals, and integers).;
<convert a rational number to a decimal>: Converting a rational number to a decimal involves expressing a fraction (a/b, where a and b are integers and b ≠ 0) as a decimal number through division.;
<solve applied problems involving all operations of rational numbers>: The ability to apply addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division to rational numbers (fractions, decimals, and integers) in real-world contexts to find solutions to practical problems.;
<compute unit rates with fractions>: A unit rate with fractions is a ratio that compares two different quantities where at least one quantity is expressed as a fraction, and the rate is simplified to a per-unit basis (e.g., cost per pound, miles per gallon).;
<identify proportional relationships>: A proportional relationship exists when two quantities vary in such a way that their ratio remains constant; this is mathematically represented as y = kx, where k is the constant of proportionality.;
<identify the constant of proportionality>: The constant of proportionality is the ratio between two directly proportional quantities, represented as k in the equation y = kx, where y and x are proportional variables.;
<represent proportional relationships with equations>: A proportional relationship is a type of linear relationship where two quantities vary directly in proportion to each other, and it can be represented by an equation of the form y = kx, where k is the constant of proportionality.;
<interpret points on proportional graphs>: Interpreting points on proportional graphs involves analyzing coordinate pairs (x, y) on a graph that represents a proportional relationship, where y = kx and the graph passes through the origin (0,0).;
<use proportional relationships to solve ratio & percent problems>: Proportional relationships are mathematical relationships between two quantities where their ratio remains constant; they are used to solve ratio and percent problems by setting up equivalent fractions or using cross-multiplication to find unknown values.;
<interpret methods of sampling>: Sampling methods refer to the techniques used to select a subset of individuals from a population to estimate characteristics of the whole population, as defined by Cochran (1977) in *Sampling Techniques*.;
<use random samples to draw influences>: Using random sampling to estimate or infer causal or behavioral influences in systems, particularly in statistics, machine learning, and social sciences, where observed patterns from random subsets are used to generalize about broader populations or underlying mechanisms.;
<compare populations using center & variability>: Comparing populations using center and variability involves analyzing and contrasting the central tendencies (e.g., mean, median) and spread (e.g., standard deviation, interquartile range) of two or more data distributions to draw meaningful inferences about differences or similarities between groups.;
<draw inferences using measures of center & variability>: Drawing inferences using measures of center (e.g., mean, median) and variability (e.g., range, standard deviation) involves using sample data to make educated conclusions about a larger population, based on central tendency and spread of the data.;
<understand that probability is between 0-1 & describes the likelihood of an event occurring>: Probability is a measure of the likelihood that an event will occur, expressed as a number between 0 and 1, where 0 indicates impossibility and 1 indicates certainty (as defined by the axioms of probability theory, e.g., Kolmogorov).;
<approximate probability of an event by collecting data>: The approximate probability of an event is estimated by observing the relative frequency of the event's occurrence in a series of repeated trials or collected data, based on the frequentist interpretation of probability.;
<develop a uniform probabilty model of a single event>: A uniform probability model assigns equal probabilities to all possible outcomes of a single event, assuming each outcome is equally likely, as defined in probability theory (e.g., Ross, 2019, 'A First Course in Probability').;
<develop a probability model of a single event>: A probability model of a single event assigns numerical probabilities to all possible outcomes of that event, satisfying the axioms of probability (non-negativity, normalization, and additivity).;
<understand probability of a compound event>: The probability of a compound event is the likelihood that two or more independent or dependent events occur simultaneously or in sequence, calculated using rules such as addition and multiplication based on event relationships.;
<represent sample spaces for compound events>: A sample space for a compound event is the set of all possible outcomes resulting from the combination of two or more individual events, often represented using organized structures like lists, tables, or tree diagrams.;
<design & use a simulation for compound events>: A simulation of compound events is a modeled representation of multiple interdependent outcomes from two or more random processes, used to estimate probabilities and analyze complex scenarios through repeated trials.;
<know & apply properties of exponents>: The properties of exponents are mathematical rules that govern how expressions with powers are simplified, multiplied, divided, and raised to additional powers, such as the product rule, quotient rule, power of a power, and zero/negative exponent rules.;
<evaluate square & cube roots>: Evaluating square roots involves finding a number that, when squared, equals the original number; evaluating cube roots involves finding a number that, when cubed, equals the original number. These are inverse operations of squaring and cubing, respectively.;
<expressing quantities in scientific notation>: Scientific notation is a standardized way of expressing numbers as a product of a coefficient (between 1 and 10) and a power of 10, enabling concise representation of very large or very small quantities.;
<apply scientific notation operations>: Scientific notation operations involve performing arithmetic (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) on numbers expressed in scientific notation, where a number is written as a product of a coefficient (between 1 and 10) and a power of 10.;
<compare slopes of proportional relationships>: Comparing slopes of proportional relationships involves analyzing the rate of change between two variables in direct proportion, where the ratio of change is constant and represented by the slope of a line through the origin.;
<find slope of a line using similar triangles>: The slope of a line can be determined by using the ratio of vertical change to horizontal change between two points, leveraging similar triangles to show that this ratio remains constant across any two points on the line.;
<solve linear equations with one, multiple or no solutions>: Solving linear equations involves finding the value(s) of variables that satisfy a given equation of the form ax + b = 0, where a and b are constants. The solution set can be one unique solution, infinitely many solutions (if equations are dependent), or no solution (if equations are inconsistent).;
<solve multi-step linear equations with rational number coefficients>: Solving multi-step linear equations with rational number coefficients involves isolating the variable through a sequence of algebraic operations, where coefficients are fractions or decimals (rational numbers), not just integers.;
<identify solutions to systems of equations using graphs>: Graphical solution of a system of equations involves plotting each equation on the same coordinate plane and identifying the point(s) of intersection, which represent the solution(s) to the system.;
<solve systems of linear equations algebraically>: Solving systems of linear equations algebraically involves finding values for variables that satisfy all equations in the system simultaneously using methods such as substitution, elimination, or matrix operations.;
<solve systems of linear equations word problems>: Solving systems of linear equations word problems involves translating real-world scenarios into mathematical equations with multiple variables, then finding the values that satisfy all equations simultaneously.;
<understand definition of a function>: A function is a relation between a set of inputs (domain) and a set of permissible outputs (codomain), where each input is related to exactly one output.;
<compare functions in different forms>: Comparing functions in different forms refers to analyzing and evaluating the same mathematical function expressed in various representations—such as algebraic, graphical, tabular, or verbal—to understand their behavior, properties, and equivalences.;
<identify linear & non-linear functions>: A linear function is a mathematical relationship between two variables that produces a straight line when graphed, typically in the form f(x) = mx + b. A non-linear function does not form a straight line and includes relationships with powers, exponents, logarithms, or trigonometric operations.;
<use functions to model relationships between quantities>: Functions are mathematical entities that assign exactly one output value to each input value, enabling the modeling of how one quantity depends on another in a precise and predictable way.;
<qualitatively describe & graph relationships>: Qualitatively describe and graph relationships refers to the process of analyzing and visually representing the nature, direction, and patterns of connections between variables or entities without relying on numerical data or quantitative measurements.;
<apply properties of transformations to lines & line segments>: Transformations of lines and line segments involve geometric operations—such as translation, rotation, reflection, and dilation—that preserve specific properties (e.g., length, parallelism, angle measures) while changing position, orientation, or size.;
<apply the properties of transformations to angles>: Applying the properties of transformations to angles involves understanding how geometric transformations—such as translations, rotations, reflections, and dilations—affect the measure, orientation, and position of angles in a plane.;
<apply the properties of transformations to parallel lines>: The application of geometric transformations—such as translations, rotations, reflections, and dilations—to parallel lines, preserving their parallelism under rigid and non-rigid transformations.;
<understand & describe geometric transformation sequences>: Geometric transformation sequences refer to ordered series of operations—such as translation, rotation, scaling, and reflection—that are applied to geometric objects to alter their position, orientation, or size in space.;
<describe the effect of transformations on 2d figures>: Transformations on 2D figures refer to geometric operations—such as translation, rotation, reflection, and dilation—that change the position, orientation, or size of a figure while preserving its essential properties (e.g., shape and angles).;
<use transformations to determine similarity in 2d figures>: Two-dimensional figures are similar if one can be transformed into the other through a sequence of rigid motions (translations, rotations, reflections) followed by a dilation, preserving shape but not necessarily size.;
<use informal arguments to establish facts about angles for similar triangles>: Informal arguments to establish facts about angles in similar triangles involve reasoning based on visual patterns, proportional relationships, and intuitive geometric insights—without relying on formal proofs or rigid deductive structures—to conclude that corresponding angles are congruent and sides are proportional.;
<prove the pythagorean theorem & it's converse>: The Pythagorean Theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides (a² + b² = c²). Its converse states that if a² + b² = c², then the triangle is a right triangle.;
<apply the pythagorean theorem to 2d & 3d figures>: The Pythagorean Theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides (a² + b² = c²). It extends to 2D and 3D figures by enabling distance calculations between points or diagonals in geometric shapes.;
<apply the pythagorean theorem to find the distance between points on the coordinate plane>: The Pythagorean Theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides. When applied to the coordinate plane, it allows calculation of the straight-line distance between two points using their coordinates.;
<know & use volume formulas>: Volume formulas are mathematical expressions used to calculate the three-dimensional space occupied by a geometric solid, such as cubes, spheres, cylinders, and prisms.;
<understand & approximate rational & irrational numbers>: Rational numbers are those that can be expressed as a fraction of two integers (p/q, where q ≠ 0), while irrational numbers cannot be expressed as such and have non-repeating, non-terminating decimal expansions. This concept involves recognizing, comparing, and approximating both types within real number systems.;
<approximate irrational numbers>: Approximate irrational numbers are decimal representations of irrational numbers that are rounded to a finite number of digits, providing a close but not exact value (e.g., π ≈ 3.14159).;
<construct & interpret scatter plots>: A scatter plot is a graphical representation that displays the relationship between two quantitative variables using dots, where each dot corresponds to an observation. It is used to identify patterns, trends, and correlations in data.;
<determine line of best fit for scatter plots>: The line of best fit is a straight line that best represents the relationship between two variables in a scatter plot, determined by minimizing the sum of the squared vertical distances (residuals) between observed data points and the line, using least squares regression.;
<use linear equations applied to bivariate data>: Linear equations applied to bivariate data involve modeling the relationship between two variables using a straight-line equation (y = mx + b), where one variable is predicted based on the other, assuming a linear association.;
<construct & interpret two way tables>: A two-way table (or contingency table) is a statistical tool that displays the frequency distribution of two categorical variables, showing how the categories of one variable relate to the categories of another.;


