<compare fractions with different numerators & denominators>: Comparing fractions with different numerators and denominators involves determining which fraction is larger or smaller by using equivalent fractions, common denominators, or cross-multiplication to make the comparison valid and accurate.;
<convert among standard measurement units>: The process of transforming a quantity from one standard unit of measurement to another within the same system (e.g., metric) or across systems (e.g., metric to imperial), using defined conversion factors.;
<create line plots with fractions>: Creating line plots with fractions involves representing fractional data points on a number line to visualize their distribution, frequency, and relationships, commonly used in elementary mathematics education.;
<understand base ten place value relationships>: Base ten place value relationships refer to the systematic organization of digits in numbers where each position represents a power of ten, enabling the representation of quantities through positional notation.;
<add & subtract fractions with unlike denominators>: Adding and subtracting fractions with unlike denominators involves finding a common denominator (typically the least common denominator) to convert fractions into equivalent forms with the same denominator, then performing the operation on the numerators while keeping the denominator unchanged.;
<solve word problems involving addition/subtraction of fractions>: Solving word problems involving addition and subtraction of fractions requires interpreting real-world scenarios, identifying fractional quantities, and applying arithmetic operations with attention to common denominators and simplification to derive accurate solutions.;
